// throttle.nv — when a coalescing redraw loop owes the screen a frame.
//
// Reading every byte of pending input before painting is the standard anti-lag
// shape for a terminal editor, and it is correct only for as long as the input
// queue actually drains.  Under key auto-repeat it need not: the terminal can
// refill stdin as fast as the loop empties it, and then "paint once the queue
// is empty" degrades into "paint once the user lets go" — a held arrow scrolls
// a list invisibly and the display jumps to the final position on release.
//
// So coalescing is bounded by TIME instead of by queue-emptiness: sustained
// input still gets frames, at display cadence rather than one per key.
//
// Pure.  No [io], no clock read — both timestamps are supplied by the caller,
// which is what makes the whole decision table assertable without a terminal.

// One frame at 30 Hz, in milliseconds: the longest a moved selection may stay
// unpainted while input is still queued.
//
// The usable band is about 16 ms (60 Hz) to 33 ms (30 Hz).  Below it the loop
// produces frames faster than any display presents them, spending on redraw the
// time it should spend on input.  Above it a held key stops reading as motion
// and starts reading as a jump.  33 ms is the slow end of that band on purpose:
// it is the cheapest cadence that still reads as continuous, so it buys the
// smoothness for the least repaint work per second of held key.
fn frame_budget_ms() -> Int
    33
@intent("""Whether a coalescing input loop must interrupt its drain and paint.

    Three inputs decide it.  `selection_moved` is the only reason to paint at all —
    a loop that has consumed input without changing what the screen would show has
    nothing to present.  `input_pending` separates the two ways a drain ends: with
    the queue empty the loop is about to fall out of the drain and paint anyway, so
    the budget is irrelevant and the answer is false; with input still queued the
    loop would otherwise keep coalescing, and the budget is the only thing that
    stops it doing so for as long as the key repeats.  The elapsed time is measured
    from the END of the previous frame, so a loop that is already keeping up (one
    frame per drained batch) is never asked to paint more often than it already
    does — the budget can only add frames a starved loop was not going to produce.

    `now_ms` and `last_render_ms` must come from the same monotonic clock;
    a wall-clock difference is not a duration.""")
fn should_render(now_ms: Int, last_render_ms: Int, budget_ms: Int, input_pending: Bool,
                 selection_moved: Bool) -> Bool
    if not selection_moved
        return false
    if not input_pending
        return false
    now_ms - last_render_ms >= budget_ms
